{"id": "rk001", "query": "what does days on market mean?", "source": "Real Estate Data Analyst Primer", "answerable": true, "gold_answer": "Days on Market (DOM) is how long a property has been listed before going under contract or selling, counted as the calendar days between the ListingContractDate and the date the status changed to Pending or the CloseDate.", "note": "handbook deliverable q"}
{"id": "rk002", "query": "wut is dom?", "source": "Real Estate Data Analyst Primer", "answerable": true, "gold_answer": "DOM stands for Days on Market: how long a property has been listed before going under contract or selling, counted as calendar days from the ListingContractDate to the Pending date or CloseDate.", "note": "typo/shorthand variant of rk001"}
{"id": "rk003", "query": "what columns are in california_sold?", "source": "MLS Schema Reference", "answerable": true, "gold_answer": "california_sold has 46 columns using RESO standard names, covering the transaction (ListingKey, ClosePrice, CloseDate, ListPrice, OriginalListPrice, DaysOnMarket, ListingContractDate, PurchaseContractDate), location (City, PostalCode, StateOrProvince, UnparsedAddress, Latitude, Longitude), the property (PropertyType, PropertySubType, LivingArea, lot size, BedroomsTotal, BathroomsTotalInteger, YearBuilt, GarageSpaces, AssociationFee, SubdivisionName, schools, True/False feature flags such as PoolPrivateYN and ViewYN), and the agents and offices on both sides.", "note": "handbook deliverable q"}
{"id": "rk004", "query": "what is a list-to-close ratio?", "source": "Real Estate Data Analyst Primer", "answerable": true, "gold_answer": "The list-to-close ratio (also called the sale-to-list ratio) is ClosePrice divided by ListPrice. A ratio above 1.0 means the home closed above asking, signalling a competitive seller's market. Below 1.0 signals buyer leverage or an overpriced listing. Example: $901,000 / $875,000 = 1.03, sold 3% over asking.", "note": "handbook deliverable q"}
{"id": "rk005", "query": "what is a comp in real estate?", "source": "Real Estate Data Analyst Primer", "answerable": true, "gold_answer": "The primer only mentions comps in passing: multi-family properties are priced on income potential (cap rate) as much as comps, and comps and analytics should be computed by matching on city and postal code because active and sold records do not share IDs. It does not give a standalone definition of a comp.", "note": ""}
{"id": "rk006", "query": "what is price per square foot?", "source": "Real Estate Data Analyst Primer", "answerable": true, "gold_answer": "The primer does not define price per square foot directly. It notes that price per sq ft differs significantly between condos and single-family homes, and that you should always filter by PropertyType before calculating median price, price per sq ft, or DOM, or the metric is skewed by what mix sold that month.", "note": ""}
{"id": "rk007", "query": "whats escrow", "source": "Real Estate Data Analyst Primer", "answerable": true, "gold_answer": "Escrow is a neutral third-party account, managed by a title company or escrow officer, that holds the buyer's deposit and all transaction funds until every condition of the sale has been met. Escrow opens once the purchase agreement is signed, and the closing window allows time for inspection, appraisal, loan underwriting, title search, and final paperwork.", "note": ""}
{"id": "rk008", "query": "whats a contingency", "source": "Real Estate Data Analyst Primer", "answerable": true, "gold_answer": "A contingency is a condition written into the purchase agreement that must be met for the deal to proceed. The three most common are the inspection contingency (buyer can cancel or renegotiate if the inspection reveals significant issues), the appraisal contingency (protects the buyer if the appraisal comes in below the agreed price), and the financing contingency (lets the buyer exit if their loan falls through).", "note": ""}
{"id": "rk009", "query": "explain pending", "source": "Real Estate Data Analyst Primer", "answerable": true, "gold_answer": "Pending means the property is under contract but not yet closed: an offer has been accepted and the purchase agreement signed, and the listing does not yet have a ClosePrice.", "note": "primer's MLS Status Codes section defines Pending - verified at labeling time"}
{"id": "rk010", "query": "difference between condo and townhouse", "source": "Real Estate Data Analyst Primer", "answerable": true, "gold_answer": "A condo is a unit within a multi-unit building where the owner holds the interior unit and common areas are shared, subject to HOA fees. A townhouse is an attached, usually multi-story home that shares one or two walls with neighbors and may have a small yard, a hybrid between single-family and condo.", "note": ""}
{"id": "rk011", "query": "explain diff between preapproval and escrow", "source": "Real Estate Data Analyst Primer", "answerable": true, "gold_answer": "Preapproval happens before an offer: a lender confirms the buyer's borrowing capacity based on income, credit score, and debt-to-income ratio, and sellers often require the preapproval letter before accepting an offer. Escrow happens after the offer is accepted and the purchase agreement is signed: a neutral third party holds the buyer's deposit and transaction funds until every condition is met and the sale closes.", "note": "two-concept query — check both retrieved"}
{"id": "rk012", "query": "what does the PoolPrivateYN field mean?", "source": "Trestle Property Metadata", "answerable": true, "gold_answer": "PoolPrivateYN is a Boolean field meaning the property has a privately owned pool that is included in the sale or lease.", "note": "gold Trestle source ranks 3rd-4th behind schema ref — passes recall@4, drags context precision"}
{"id": "rk013", "query": "what kind of field is ListingKey?", "source": "Trestle Property Metadata", "answerable": true, "gold_answer": "ListingKey is a String field that uniquely identifies the record from the immediate source; it is the local key of the system.", "note": ""}
{"id": "rk014", "query": "how do rets_property and california_sold join?", "source": "MLS Schema Reference", "answerable": true, "gold_answer": "They do not join on listing ID: joining rets_property.L_ListingID to california_sold.ListingKey returns 0 rows, because ListingKey and ListingId are different fields and active and sold records do not share IDs. The working join is market-level: rets_property.L_City = california_sold.City and rets_property.L_Zip = california_sold.PostalCode, which is the join to use for comps and analytics.", "note": "comp-join, not identity join"}
{"id": "rk015", "query": "what does StandardStatus mean?", "source": "Trestle Property Metadata", "answerable": true, "gold_answer": "StandardStatus is the status of the listing as it reflects the state of the contract between the listing agent and seller, or an agreement with a buyer. Values are Active, Active Under Contract, Canceled, Closed, Expired, Pending, and Withdrawn.", "note": ""}
{"id": "rk016", "query": "what is ClosePrice?", "source": "Real Estate Data Analyst Primer", "answerable": true, "gold_answer": "ClosePrice is what the market will pay: the true transaction value, which should be used for price trend analysis rather than ListPrice.", "note": "primer outranks Trestle on retrieval, so sourced to the primer"}
{"id": "rk017", "query": "whats the difference between ListPrice and OriginalListPrice", "source": "Trestle Property Metadata", "answerable": true, "gold_answer": "ListPrice is the current price of the property as determined by the seller and the seller's broker. OriginalListPrice is the original price on the initial agreement between the seller and the seller's broker.", "note": ""}
{"id": "rk018", "query": "define LivingArea", "source": "Trestle Property Metadata", "answerable": true, "gold_answer": "LivingArea is the total livable area within the structure.", "note": ""}
{"id": "rk019", "query": "what is the AssociationFee field?", "source": "Trestle Property Metadata", "answerable": true, "gold_answer": "AssociationFee is a fee paid by the homeowner to the Home Owners Association, used for the upkeep of the common area, neighborhood, or other association-related benefits.", "note": ""}
{"id": "rk020", "query": "what does ListingContractDate mean", "source": "Trestle Property Metadata", "answerable": true, "gold_answer": "ListingContractDate is the effective date of the agreement between the seller and the seller's broker, entered by the agent to reflect when the change occurred contractually, not a timestamp of when it was made in the MLS.", "note": ""}
{"id": "rk021", "query": "what is CloseDate in trestle", "source": "Trestle Property Metadata", "answerable": true, "gold_answer": "For for-sale listings, CloseDate is the date the purchase agreement was fulfilled.", "note": ""}
{"id": "rk022", "query": "what does PurchaseContractDate represent?", "source": "Trestle Property Metadata", "answerable": true, "gold_answer": "For for-sale listings, PurchaseContractDate is the date an offer was accepted and the listing was no longer on market, entered by the agent to reflect when the change occurred contractually.", "note": ""}
{"id": "rk023", "query": "explain the LotSizeAcres field", "source": "Trestle Property Metadata", "answerable": true, "gold_answer": "LotSizeAcres is the total acres of the lot. It is related to Lot Size Area and Lot Size Units and must stay in sync with those fields.", "note": ""}
{"id": "rk024", "query": "what is PropertySubType", "source": "Trestle Property Metadata", "answerable": true, "gold_answer": "PropertySubType is a list of sub types to Residential, Residential Lease, Manufactured in Park, Commercial and Business Opportunity listings, for example Single Family Residence, Condominium, Manufactured on Land, Townhouse, Multi Family, Office, or Retail.", "note": ""}
{"id": "rk025", "query": "what does the ViewYN field indicate", "source": "Trestle Property Metadata", "answerable": true, "gold_answer": "ViewYN is a Boolean indicating the property has a view.", "note": ""}
{"id": "rk026", "query": "what is NewConstructionYN?", "source": "Trestle Property Metadata", "answerable": true, "gold_answer": "NewConstructionYN is a Boolean indicating whether the property is newly constructed and has not been previously occupied.", "note": ""}
{"id": "rk027", "query": "what does SubdivisionName mean", "source": "Trestle Property Metadata", "answerable": true, "gold_answer": "SubdivisionName is a neighborhood, community, complex, or builder tract.", "note": ""}
{"id": "rk028", "query": "how is ListingId different from ListingKey?", "source": "Trestle Property Metadata", "answerable": true, "gold_answer": "ListingId is the well-known identifier for the listing, intended to be the value a human uses to retrieve information about a specific listing. Its value may be identical to the ListingKey, but ListingKey is the system's unique local key.", "note": "two-concept query"}
{"id": "rk029", "query": "what is ModificationTimestamp", "source": "Trestle Property Metadata", "answerable": true, "gold_answer": "ModificationTimestamp is the transactional timestamp automatically recorded by the MLS system representing the date and time the listing was last modified.", "note": ""}
{"id": "rk030", "query": "how do u calculate days on market", "source": "Real Estate Data Analyst Primer", "answerable": true, "gold_answer": "Days on Market (DOM) is how long a property has been listed before going under contract or selling, counted as the calendar days between the ListingContractDate and the date the status changed to Pending or the CloseDate.", "note": "variant of rk001"}
{"id": "rk031", "query": "whats sale to list ratio mean", "source": "Real Estate Data Analyst Primer", "answerable": true, "gold_answer": "The list-to-close ratio (also called the sale-to-list ratio) is ClosePrice divided by ListPrice. A ratio above 1.0 means the home closed above asking, signalling a competitive seller's market. Below 1.0 signals buyer leverage or an overpriced listing. Example: $901,000 / $875,000 = 1.03, sold 3% over asking.", "note": "variant of rk004 using the primer's own term"}
