{"id": "rk038", "query": "can i join active listings to sold ones on listing id", "source": "MLS Schema Reference", "answerable": true, "gold_answer": "They do not join on listing ID: joining rets_property.L_ListingID to california_sold.ListingKey returns 0 rows, because ListingKey and ListingId are different fields and active and sold records do not share IDs. The working join is market-level: rets_property.L_City = california_sold.City and rets_property.L_Zip = california_sold.PostalCode, which is the join to use for comps and analytics.", "note": "variant of rk014"}
{"id": "rk039", "query": "list the fields in the sold table", "source": "MLS Schema Reference", "answerable": true, "gold_answer": "california_sold has 46 columns using RESO standard names, covering the transaction (ListingKey, ClosePrice, CloseDate, ListPrice, OriginalListPrice, DaysOnMarket, ListingContractDate, PurchaseContractDate), location (City, PostalCode, StateOrProvince, UnparsedAddress, Latitude, Longitude), the property (PropertyType, PropertySubType, LivingArea, lot size, BedroomsTotal, BathroomsTotalInteger, YearBuilt, GarageSpaces, AssociationFee, SubdivisionName, schools, True/False feature flags such as PoolPrivateYN and ViewYN), and the agents and offices on both sides.", "note": "variant of rk003"}
{"id": "rk040", "query": "why do i need to filter CloseDate before averaging?", "source": "MLS Schema Reference", "answerable": true, "gold_answer": "CloseDate is stored as VARCHAR rather than DateTime in the local import, so validation was dropped and the table contains 4 impossible future dates such as 2072-06-29. Filter WHERE CloseDate <= CURDATE() before any date math or averaging.", "note": ""}
{"id": "rk041", "query": "what values do the YN flags like PoolPrivateYN store in the database?", "source": "MLS Schema Reference", "answerable": true, "gold_answer": "The YN flags store '1' for yes and empty or NULL for no or unknown, not 'True'/'False'. Filtering PoolPrivateYN = 'True' matches 0 rows; filter = '1' instead.", "note": ""}
{"id": "rk042", "query": "how do i filter for single family homes in california_sold", "source": "MLS Schema Reference", "answerable": true, "gold_answer": "PropertyType is 100% Residential in california_sold, so filtering on it does nothing. Use PropertySubType = 'SingleFamilyResidence' for the single-family benchmark.", "note": ""}
{"id": "rk043", "query": "why do the two tables use different column names?", "source": "MLS Schema Reference", "answerable": true, "gold_answer": "rets_property uses legacy vendor names such as L_* and LM_*, while california_sold uses RESO standard names. Don't assume a column in one table exists by the same name in the other.", "note": ""}
{"id": "rk044", "query": "how many rows are in rets_property and california_sold", "source": "MLS Schema Reference", "answerable": true, "gold_answer": "The local import has 53,122 rows in rets_property (active listings) and 87,157 rows in california_sold (sold transactions). The handbook's larger counts are wrong; the local database is the source of truth.", "note": ""}
{"id": "rk045", "query": "why use NULLIF on the sqft column in rets_property", "source": "MLS Schema Reference", "answerable": true, "gold_answer": "In rets_property, 192 rows have a square footage (LM_Int2_3) of 0 or NULL, so use NULLIF(LM_Int2_3, 0) when computing averages.", "note": ""}
